{
 "id": "2018-van-greenery",
 "name": "Greenery in University Spaces",
 "description": "Design-variant experiment from van den Bogerd, Dijkstra, Seidell & Maas (2018, PLOS ONE). 722 Dutch students each saw one randomly assigned version of four university spaces (lecture hall, classroom, study area, outdoor space). Each version was the same photo, digitally edited by an architect to add a colourful poster, a nature poster, a green wall, interior plants, seating, colourful artefacts or greenery. Students rated preference (the setting is pleasant / attractive / I would like to be educated in, study in or stay in this setting) on a 0–4 agree scale. For the outdoor space they also rated perceived restoration likelihood (come to rest and renewed energy after 20 minutes; regaining concentration after an exam week). Rooms with a nature poster, green wall or plants, and outdoor spaces with greenery, scored higher than the standard and colourful designs. People who called themselves nature lovers rated everything higher. The template ships with the ten design photos printed in the paper's Figs 1–2 (CC BY, Burton Hamfelt Architects): all four outdoor designs, and two designs each for the lecture hall (colourful poster, green wall), the classroom (standard, green wall plus plants) and the study area (nature poster, green wall). Each space has its own media folder, so you can add your own design variants. The page records which design each person saw (assigned_designs), and every rating is tagged with its photo. Analysis as in the paper: compare designs within each space (the paper used multilevel regression with students nested in universities); split by the 1–10 \"nature lover\" item (8 or higher = strong connectedness). Differences from the original study: (1) only the 10 designs shown in the paper's figures are included; the paper had 4–5 designs per indoor space (14 indoor designs in total), so 8 indoor designs are missing; (2) panel letters were cropped from the figure photos; (3) the questionnaire's extra items (focus and creative thinking for indoor spaces; socializing and physical activity outdoors) are kept, as in the original questionnaire, though the paper did not analyse them; (4) the long sections on lifestyle and the student's current campus are left out; (5) an \"Other / prefer not to say\" gender option is added.",
